Halloween has its origins in the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain, which was celebrated on the evening of October 31st. The Celts, who lived about 2,000 years ago in the area that is now Ireland, the United Kingdom, and northern France, marked Samhain as a day that marked the end of the harvest season and the beginning of winter, a time often associated with human death.

The Celts believed that on the night of Samhain, the boundary between the living and the dead was blurred, allowing spirits of the deceased to return to earth. To honor these spirits, they would light bonfires and wear costumes. Villagers would also leave offerings of food and drink to attract benevolent spirits and ensure a good harvest.

With the spread of Christianity throughout Europe, the Church sought to replace pagan traditions with Christian ones. In the 8th century, Pope Gregory III designated November 1st as All Saints' Day (or All Hallows), a day to honor saints and martyrs. October 31st became known as All Hallows' Eve, which eventually evolved into the term "Halloween".

